{#
    One card, and the title in it is the link.

    A card wrapped in one anchor announces its whole contents as that link's
    name, and takes selecting the text inside it away from the reader. So what
    is pressable is the line that says where pressing it goes, and the card
    follows on hover — which is what makes the whole thing feel like the target
    it deliberately is not. Where there is nowhere to go, the title is a title.

    **The element, not a hand-built card.** `sds-teaser` is the front door and
    a `div` with its classes on it is the fallback, which is the rule for every
    other node this theme writes — and it is what makes the card the element's
    to change: the frame around it is drawn once, in one file, and a page the
    renderer produced cannot drift from a page a product wrote.

    The parts are still written here, between the tags, and the element keeps
    them. That is the theme's other rule — the server writes, the element
    upgrades — and a card is where it bites hardest: a title, a picture and a
    summary that waited for a script would leave a reader with none looking at
    an empty box in a grid of them. What arrives through an attribute is what
    an attribute can carry, and a card is made of none of it: the title may
    have resolved to a link, the summary is paragraphs.

    So the parts carry the classes the element itself emits for them — the
    markers it reads its own card back by, and the same ones `sds-figure` and
    `sds-code` use. Node for node is the whole card and not the half of it a
    landing page happened to need first.

    **The badge is written as its class rather than as `sds-badge`.** That
    element renders its span from a property, so one written here would be an
    empty row until a script ran, and empty for good on a page that runs none.
    It costs nothing: the badge and the label have no behaviour, so the class
    layer is the whole of them.
#}
{#- The picture goes by the name every other directive that takes a file uses:
    what an option is called belongs to the language it is written in, and
    `.. figure::` and `.. image::` already gave the file that name. -#}
{%- set src = node.option('src') -%}
{%- set alt = node.option('alt')|default('') -%}
<sds-teaser>
    {%- if src %}
        {#- The picture, referenced or linked by what it is — see
            `body/figure.html.twig`, which decides it the same way and says
            why: a drawing referenced into the page takes the page's tokens
            and follows it into dark, an `<img>` renders in a document of its
            own where none are declared. A file somewhere else is linked
            whatever it is, because a reference reads the file itself and a
            browser will not do that across origins. -#}
        {%- set external = src is external_target -%}
        {%- set source = external ? src : asset(src) -%}
        <div class="sds-teaser__image">
            {%- if not external and src ends with '.svg' -%}
                {#- Named where there is something to say, and hidden where
                    there is not: an unlabelled picture and a decorative one
                    are read out very differently, and a card whose art
                    repeats the title beside it is the second. -#}
                <svg class="sds-art"{% if alt %} role="img" aria-label="{{ alt }}"{% else %} aria-hidden="true"{% endif %}><use href="{{ source }}#art"></use></svg>
            {%- else -%}
                <img class="sds-art" src="{{ source }}" alt="{{ alt }}">
            {%- endif -%}
        </div>
    {%- endif %}
    <div class="sds-teaser__body">
        {#- The row is dropped rather than left empty: a card whose first line
            is blank is a card with a hole where a set of them lines up. -#}
        {%- if node.option('tag') or node.option('meta') %}
            <div class="sds-row">
                {%- if node.option('tag') %}<span class="sds-badge">{{ node.option('tag') }}</span>{% endif -%}
                {%- if node.option('meta') %} <span class="sds-label">{{ node.option('meta') }}</span>{% endif -%}
            </div>
        {%- endif %}
        {%- if node.option('title') %}
            <h3 class="sds-teaser__title">
                {%- if node.option('href') -%}
                    <a href="{{ renderLink(node.option('href')) }}">{{ node.option('title') }}</a>
                {%- else -%}
                    {{ node.option('title') }}
                {%- endif -%}
            </h3>
        {%- endif %}
        <div class="sds-teaser__text">
            {%- for child in node.children -%}
                {{ renderNode(child) }}
            {%- endfor -%}
        </div>
    </div>
</sds-teaser>
